  "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100085/assets/a5d544a1c4c7d0227e464aae92f8b282f716fa41ee57daa1061e77e1898a8a00.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}{e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
